# `Serve`

<SymbolMeta />
<SymbolSignature />

`Serve<E>` attaches a built [`ProtocolRuntime`](./ProtocolRuntime) to one concrete endpoint type `E`. Its `serve` method receives the runtime, `AppRuntime`, a `ShutdownSignal`, and the endpoint, then returns the endpoint-serving future.

## Ownership contract

The implementation owns endpoint resources and protocol-spawned work. On shutdown it must stop accepting new work and complete its documented drain or cancellation behavior before the future resolves. It should not initiate application shutdown itself.

The caller owns the application lifecycle envelope: startup hooks, reload coordination, shutdown initiation, and final cleanup. `Serve` therefore watches the supplied signal rather than owning a `ShutdownHandle`. This boundary allows an application to host one runtime through several endpoint types while preserving one shutdown authority.

Construct the runtime in [`PreparedProtocol::build`](./PreparedProtocol), not in `serve`. See [the custom protocol guide](/docs/latest/framework/extensions/protocol-authoring) for a staged serving example.

